1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:03,280
We're going over why taxes are bad at the logic efficiency,

2
00:00:03,280 --> 00:00:06,600
which is no way a libertarian point.

3
00:00:06,600 --> 00:00:09,360
Instead, it's a simple neoclassical point

4
00:00:09,360 --> 00:00:11,040
about how taxes work.

5
00:00:11,040 --> 00:00:12,920
Can you hit the door back there?

6
00:00:12,920 --> 00:00:14,560
So just refresh your memory.

7
00:00:14,560 --> 00:00:17,160
Imagine we've got shirts that are the marginal cost

8
00:00:17,160 --> 00:00:19,240
equals to $18.

9
00:00:19,240 --> 00:00:22,960
And you are willing to pay $20.

10
00:00:22,960 --> 00:00:26,840
You're willing to pay just one unit.

11
00:00:26,840 --> 00:00:29,240
So one unit, one shirt.

12
00:00:29,240 --> 00:00:32,600
You are at no limit to pay $20.

13
00:00:32,600 --> 00:00:33,100
All right.

14
00:00:33,100 --> 00:00:34,440
That's the supply.

15
00:00:34,440 --> 00:00:39,080
And then, if there is no tax, then the price will be $18.

16
00:00:39,080 --> 00:00:40,120
And you value $20.

17
00:00:40,120 --> 00:00:42,560
And therefore, you buy.

18
00:00:42,560 --> 00:00:43,640
Yes.

19
00:00:43,640 --> 00:00:45,480
And how much consumer surplus do you get?

20
00:00:45,480 --> 00:00:45,980
$2.

21
00:00:45,980 --> 00:00:46,480
$2.

22
00:00:46,480 --> 00:00:48,440
And how much revenue does the government raise

23
00:00:48,440 --> 00:00:49,520
when there are no taxes?

24
00:00:49,520 --> 00:00:50,840
$0.

25
00:00:50,840 --> 00:00:54,800
And then what if the government raises a tax of one penny?

26
00:00:54,800 --> 00:00:56,520
The price goes up to what?

27
00:00:56,520 --> 00:00:58,160
Perfectly elastic supply.

28
00:00:58,160 --> 00:01:00,080
$18.01.

29
00:01:00,080 --> 00:01:02,120
Do you still buy when you're on space 20?

30
00:01:02,120 --> 00:01:03,120
Yes.

31
00:01:03,120 --> 00:01:05,200
How much surplus do you get?

32
00:01:05,200 --> 00:01:05,880
$1.91.

33
00:01:05,880 --> 00:01:06,720
$1.91.

34
00:01:06,720 --> 00:01:07,200
$1.96.

35
00:01:07,200 --> 00:01:08,960
How much revenue does the government raise?

36
00:01:08,960 --> 00:01:09,460
A penny.

37
00:01:09,460 --> 00:01:10,320
A penny.

38
00:01:10,320 --> 00:01:10,820
All right.

39
00:01:10,820 --> 00:01:12,880
Go up to $1.99 tax.

40
00:01:12,880 --> 00:01:14,240
Do you still buy?

41
00:01:14,240 --> 00:01:14,960
Yes.

42
00:01:14,960 --> 00:01:16,840
How much surplus do you get?

43
00:01:16,840 --> 00:01:17,340
A penny.

44
00:01:17,340 --> 00:01:19,200
How much revenue does the government get?

45
00:01:19,200 --> 00:01:19,920
$1.99.

46
00:01:19,920 --> 00:01:22,760
And then the government raises the tax to $2.01.

47
00:01:22,760 --> 00:01:23,800
Do you still buy?

48
00:01:23,800 --> 00:01:24,300
No.

49
00:01:24,300 --> 00:01:25,480
No.

50
00:01:25,480 --> 00:01:28,120
And then how much surplus do you get when you don't do anything?

51
00:01:28,120 --> 00:01:28,620
$0.

52
00:01:28,620 --> 00:01:30,160
And how much revenue does the government raise

53
00:01:30,160 --> 00:01:31,320
when you don't buy anything?

54
00:01:31,320 --> 00:01:31,820
Nothing.

55
00:01:31,820 --> 00:01:32,320
Nothing.

56
00:01:32,320 --> 00:01:34,840
And that's deadweight cost.

57
00:01:34,840 --> 00:01:37,400
That is the key insight, which, of course,

58
00:01:37,400 --> 00:01:41,120
applies for pricing, applies to monopoly pricing,

59
00:01:41,120 --> 00:01:43,360
applies to taxation.

60
00:01:43,360 --> 00:01:46,000
In anything you think of that leads there

61
00:01:46,000 --> 00:01:49,400
to be a reduction in one person's surplus

62
00:01:49,400 --> 00:01:52,120
without a corresponding increase in anyone else's surplus,

63
00:01:52,120 --> 00:01:55,520
that is our deadweight cost.

64
00:01:55,520 --> 00:01:57,760
And again, the classic version of this, of course,

65
00:01:57,760 --> 00:02:00,600
is for monopoly.

66
00:02:00,600 --> 00:02:02,760
You've got a person running a cost.

67
00:02:02,760 --> 00:02:04,200
There's the demand curve.

68
00:02:04,200 --> 00:02:05,800
There's her monopoly price.

69
00:02:05,800 --> 00:02:07,360
And there's your deadweight loss.

70
00:02:07,360 --> 00:02:09,640
But notice, we could get exactly the same result

71
00:02:09,640 --> 00:02:12,720
if we had perfect competition at a tax.

72
00:02:12,720 --> 00:02:14,920
Exactly the same idea.

73
00:02:14,920 --> 00:02:17,920
Because the tax means that rather than the assuming

74
00:02:17,920 --> 00:02:20,440
like here, when the firm goes and raises the price,

75
00:02:20,440 --> 00:02:22,800
on these units, you get less surplus.

76
00:02:22,800 --> 00:02:24,080
The firm gets more.

77
00:02:24,080 --> 00:02:27,080
But on those units, the surplus simply vanishes,

78
00:02:27,080 --> 00:02:30,480
and the monopolist doesn't get any.

79
00:02:30,480 --> 00:02:32,280
And the same would go if there were tax.

80
00:02:35,000 --> 00:02:37,520
Now we have a special name for this kind of inefficiency.

81
00:02:37,520 --> 00:02:39,040
It's called allocative inefficiency.

82
00:02:42,520 --> 00:02:47,080
And at least for allocative inefficiency

83
00:02:47,080 --> 00:02:48,800
on the free market, when people finally

84
00:02:48,800 --> 00:02:51,120
got around to actually estimating the size,

85
00:02:51,120 --> 00:02:53,640
because of course, we could go and draw triangles for decades

86
00:02:53,640 --> 00:02:55,800
without ever coming up with a number.

87
00:02:55,800 --> 00:02:58,400
And we're economists capable of doing this.

88
00:02:58,400 --> 00:03:00,840
Talk about a problem without ever measuring it.

89
00:03:00,840 --> 00:03:02,840
Yes, well, before there's any decent computers,

90
00:03:02,840 --> 00:03:05,120
it's even easier to understand why people didn't do it.

91
00:03:05,120 --> 00:03:06,760
It's like, well, let's talk about what

92
00:03:06,760 --> 00:03:11,960
we feel count-competent to talk about, and not do what's hard.

93
00:03:11,960 --> 00:03:13,800
So anyway, so you look really puzzled.

94
00:03:13,800 --> 00:03:15,360
No, no, I don't have my glasses on.

95
00:03:15,360 --> 00:03:15,860
I'm sorry.

96
00:03:15,860 --> 00:03:16,440
I can see that.

97
00:03:16,440 --> 00:03:17,880
Deadweight loss triangle.

98
00:03:17,880 --> 00:03:20,400
All right, so anyway, there was an economist, Harberger,

99
00:03:20,400 --> 00:03:22,840
who finally in the 60s, I believe,

100
00:03:22,840 --> 00:03:24,680
started coming up with estimates.

101
00:03:24,680 --> 00:03:29,360
And he came up with an estimate for all of the monopoly

102
00:03:29,360 --> 00:03:33,640
deadweight loss triangles in the entire economy equal to 0.1%

103
00:03:33,640 --> 00:03:36,560
of GDP.

104
00:03:36,560 --> 00:03:38,240
And super low number.

105
00:03:38,240 --> 00:03:40,360
But again, if you think about it just geometrically,

106
00:03:40,360 --> 00:03:41,780
it's more plausible than it seems,

107
00:03:41,780 --> 00:03:43,800
because remember, we're only losing that

108
00:03:43,800 --> 00:03:45,560
on the marginal units.

109
00:03:45,560 --> 00:03:49,120
And the marginal units aren't valued very much.

110
00:03:49,120 --> 00:03:50,880
So we only lose the marginal units.

111
00:03:50,880 --> 00:03:52,960
The marginal units aren't valued very much.

112
00:03:52,960 --> 00:03:56,520
And therefore, it's not so surprising the loss is small.

113
00:03:56,520 --> 00:03:59,000
And again, just remember the basic geometry of triangles.

114
00:03:59,000 --> 00:04:04,720
Remember the area of a triangle is 1 half base height.

115
00:04:04,720 --> 00:04:07,080
Base height.

116
00:04:07,080 --> 00:04:12,200
So if, for example, this were a, if we just had a slope of 1,

117
00:04:12,200 --> 00:04:15,400
then the area would just be 1 half base squared.

118
00:04:15,400 --> 00:04:18,760
So another key thing about deadweight loss triangles

119
00:04:18,760 --> 00:04:22,840
is that if you double the size of the price wedge,

120
00:04:22,840 --> 00:04:25,200
you quadruple the size of the loss.

121
00:04:25,200 --> 00:04:28,880
And if you increase the size of the wedge by a factor of 10,

122
00:04:28,880 --> 00:04:31,920
then you increase the loss by a factor of 100.

123
00:04:31,920 --> 00:04:34,640
So again, as long as you're only getting

124
00:04:34,640 --> 00:04:36,320
a little bit above marginal cost,

125
00:04:36,320 --> 00:04:40,000
you're getting a very tiny loss.

126
00:04:40,000 --> 00:04:43,360
Whereas if you had, say, a prohibited tax,

127
00:04:43,360 --> 00:04:44,720
taxes on the white-bottom market,

128
00:04:44,720 --> 00:04:47,680
then you could actually exploit all of that

129
00:04:47,680 --> 00:04:50,680
if you were going to try.

130
00:04:50,680 --> 00:04:52,680
So anyway, you can move this really low number.

131
00:04:52,680 --> 00:04:54,040
As you might guess, when you come up with a number,

132
00:04:54,040 --> 00:04:54,760
literally no number.

133
00:04:54,760 --> 00:04:56,600
A bunch of other people try to make the number bigger.

134
00:04:56,600 --> 00:04:58,960
And there's a series of papers trying to make it bigger.

135
00:04:58,960 --> 00:04:59,960
But it was hard.

136
00:04:59,960 --> 00:05:01,920
And then finally, there was the Samuelson-Quip,

137
00:05:01,920 --> 00:05:04,040
which I think I mentioned at the end of last class,

138
00:05:04,040 --> 00:05:06,880
which is that anything is small if you divide it by GDP.

139
00:05:06,880 --> 00:05:09,840
Which is a reasonably good joke.

140
00:05:09,840 --> 00:05:12,000
Although Samuelson, above all people,

141
00:05:12,000 --> 00:05:13,200
should know that's not true because he

142
00:05:13,200 --> 00:05:14,000
lived through the Depression.

143
00:05:14,000 --> 00:05:16,040
Is the Depression small if you divide it by GDP?

144
00:05:16,040 --> 00:05:16,560
No.

145
00:05:16,560 --> 00:05:17,280
No.

146
00:05:17,280 --> 00:05:19,040
So it's not true that everything is small

147
00:05:19,040 --> 00:05:20,840
if you divide it by GDP.

148
00:05:20,840 --> 00:05:23,560
And again, remember, this is not saying deadweight loss

149
00:05:23,560 --> 00:05:26,640
in the chewing gum industry is small if you divide it by GDP.

150
00:05:26,640 --> 00:05:28,520
The whole chewing gum industry is small

151
00:05:28,520 --> 00:05:30,200
if you divide it by GDP.

152
00:05:30,200 --> 00:05:33,480
It's saying that you take all the losses of all the monopoly

153
00:05:33,480 --> 00:05:37,000
in the entire economy and then divide it by GDP.

154
00:05:37,000 --> 00:05:38,160
That's small.

155
00:05:38,160 --> 00:05:42,440
And that's a pretty interesting result.

156
00:05:42,440 --> 00:05:45,520
All right, now, there is another source of inefficiency

157
00:05:45,520 --> 00:05:47,200
that is discussed.

158
00:05:47,200 --> 00:05:49,480
Maybe 5% is much.

159
00:05:49,480 --> 00:05:53,240
And yet the empirical estimates say that it's many times bigger.

160
00:05:53,240 --> 00:05:55,400
Why on earth would it be that we would

161
00:05:55,400 --> 00:05:57,880
talk a lot about a small problem and hardly ever discuss

162
00:05:57,880 --> 00:05:58,520
a big problem?

163
00:06:01,720 --> 00:06:03,720
Robin Hanson might have an explanation.

164
00:06:03,720 --> 00:06:06,920
Yes, discussion is not about the magnitude of problems.

165
00:06:06,920 --> 00:06:08,560
The magnitude of the discussion is not

166
00:06:08,560 --> 00:06:10,240
about the magnitude of the problem.

167
00:06:10,240 --> 00:06:12,920
Instead, mostly the magnitude of the discussion

168
00:06:12,920 --> 00:06:16,840
is about how much have people discussed in the past.

169
00:06:16,840 --> 00:06:18,280
So if other people are complaining

170
00:06:18,280 --> 00:06:19,740
about something a lot, then you want

171
00:06:19,740 --> 00:06:21,200
to complain about something a lot.

172
00:06:21,200 --> 00:06:23,680
If no one else cares, then it doesn't really

173
00:06:23,680 --> 00:06:26,000
pay for you to care, normally.

174
00:06:26,000 --> 00:06:29,320
So every now and then, you can be an intellectual entrepreneur

175
00:06:29,320 --> 00:06:32,480
and say, all of you people are scum for ignoring

176
00:06:32,480 --> 00:06:34,840
a much bigger problem.

177
00:06:34,840 --> 00:06:36,680
Sometimes that works, but usually, you're

178
00:06:36,680 --> 00:06:38,600
just talking to yourself.

179
00:06:38,600 --> 00:06:41,680
People say, man, that guy is rude.

180
00:06:41,680 --> 00:06:47,440
Calling us scum just for being obsessed with a minor problem.

181
00:06:47,440 --> 00:06:50,960
Anyhow, the much larger source of inefficiency,

182
00:06:50,960 --> 00:06:53,560
according to the empirical work that's been done,

183
00:06:53,560 --> 00:06:55,000
is called productive inefficiency.

184
00:07:00,040 --> 00:07:03,040
Also sometimes called x inefficiency.

185
00:07:03,040 --> 00:07:05,920
That's a term that was briefly around the 60s and 70s.

186
00:07:05,920 --> 00:07:07,560
When Ryan Murphy was here, I gave him

187
00:07:07,560 --> 00:07:11,160
the question of what is the most underrated topic in economics,

188
00:07:11,160 --> 00:07:13,080
and he said x inefficiency.

189
00:07:13,080 --> 00:07:15,480
Good answer.

190
00:07:15,480 --> 00:07:18,040
Anyway, so productive inefficiency

191
00:07:18,040 --> 00:07:20,760
is what you get when you're producing

192
00:07:20,760 --> 00:07:25,280
at above the minimum average cost for society.

193
00:07:25,280 --> 00:07:27,200
So you're productively inefficient if and only

194
00:07:27,200 --> 00:07:29,720
if the average cost of reducing the quantity

195
00:07:29,720 --> 00:07:33,240
is above the actual minimum average cost.

196
00:07:33,240 --> 00:07:35,000
These productive inefficiencies can easily

197
00:07:35,000 --> 00:07:38,720
be large because they exist on all units produced.

198
00:07:38,720 --> 00:07:41,400
Every unit produced at a higher than necessary cost

199
00:07:41,400 --> 00:07:45,400
can lead to a very large problem.

200
00:07:45,400 --> 00:07:48,920
So just to take a look, so suppose

201
00:07:48,920 --> 00:07:52,080
that we have no allocated inefficiency,

202
00:07:52,080 --> 00:07:55,840
so we're producing at marginal cost.

203
00:07:55,840 --> 00:07:59,680
So here is, and we'll make a flat cost curve

204
00:07:59,680 --> 00:08:02,320
so that average and marginal cost are the same.

205
00:08:02,320 --> 00:08:04,360
Remember that?

206
00:08:04,360 --> 00:08:07,600
Average cost is the key to productive inefficiency.

207
00:08:07,600 --> 00:08:11,120
So here we've got average cost one,

208
00:08:11,120 --> 00:08:13,520
and here we've got average cost two.

209
00:08:13,520 --> 00:08:15,560
And then here we've got demand.

210
00:08:15,560 --> 00:08:18,680
And then imagine that, say, that this guy is the brother-in-law

211
00:08:18,680 --> 00:08:20,240
of the dictator.

212
00:08:20,240 --> 00:08:22,480
So this guy is the brother-in-law of the dictator.

213
00:08:22,480 --> 00:08:25,120
This guy is unrelated to the dictator.

214
00:08:25,120 --> 00:08:27,440
And so the dictator says, you're not allowed to produce.

215
00:08:27,440 --> 00:08:29,000
I won't give you a license.

216
00:08:29,000 --> 00:08:31,120
So then this guy has the monopoly.

217
00:08:31,120 --> 00:08:34,600
Now imagine this guy actually produces at marginal cost.

218
00:08:34,600 --> 00:08:36,280
So he's producing right there.

219
00:08:36,280 --> 00:08:38,600
So producing at average cost equals marginal cost.

220
00:08:38,600 --> 00:08:41,000
There's no allocated inefficiency.

221
00:08:41,000 --> 00:08:45,960
But we have all of that productive inefficiency.

222
00:08:45,960 --> 00:08:50,600
And you may even remember the geometric name for that shape.

223
00:08:50,600 --> 00:08:52,480
It's a trapezoid.

224
00:08:52,480 --> 00:08:54,600
All right, a trapezoid.

225
00:08:54,600 --> 00:08:57,080
So that is the dreaded productive inefficiency

226
00:08:57,080 --> 00:08:59,800
trapezoid.

227
00:08:59,800 --> 00:09:01,360
All right, anyone ever even heard

228
00:09:01,360 --> 00:09:03,720
of the productive inefficiency trapezoid outside of this

229
00:09:03,720 --> 00:09:04,600
class?

230
00:09:04,600 --> 00:09:06,040
All right, so a couple people.

231
00:09:06,040 --> 00:09:06,540
Good.

232
00:09:06,540 --> 00:09:08,880
But anyway, which goes to my point

233
00:09:08,880 --> 00:09:10,880
about how economists hardly ever talk about it,

234
00:09:10,880 --> 00:09:12,760
even though empirical estimates say

235
00:09:12,760 --> 00:09:18,800
it's bigger by a lot than allocated inefficiency.

236
00:09:18,800 --> 00:09:21,640
All right, and that's the idea.

237
00:09:21,640 --> 00:09:23,800
And again, the idea is so from each of these units,

238
00:09:23,800 --> 00:09:25,000
we are having loss.

239
00:09:25,000 --> 00:09:27,000
And again, notice the definition just says

240
00:09:27,000 --> 00:09:29,320
all of the loss associated with the fact

241
00:09:29,320 --> 00:09:32,640
that we don't have the lowest cost producer producing.

242
00:09:32,640 --> 00:09:35,240
So sometimes people want to go and cut this up

243
00:09:35,240 --> 00:09:38,360
into a rectangle and a triangle.

244
00:09:38,360 --> 00:09:40,560
You could reclassify it all that way

245
00:09:40,560 --> 00:09:43,120
if you wanted to, but this is more elegant.

246
00:09:43,120 --> 00:09:45,320
So this is what people usually do.

247
00:09:45,320 --> 00:09:46,720
This is what they'll do.

248
00:09:46,720 --> 00:09:53,080
So you have all of that loss caused by cost being too high.

249
00:09:53,080 --> 00:09:57,600
Now, imagine here that we had contestable monopoly.

250
00:09:57,600 --> 00:10:01,120
So anyone's allowed to produce if they want to.

251
00:10:01,120 --> 00:10:02,880
So with contestable monopoly, would there

252
00:10:02,880 --> 00:10:05,320
be any productive inefficiency?

253
00:10:05,320 --> 00:10:06,720
We talked about how productive.

254
00:10:06,720 --> 00:10:07,520
Not how productive.

255
00:10:07,520 --> 00:10:08,240
It might be less.

256
00:10:08,240 --> 00:10:08,880
It might be some.

257
00:10:08,880 --> 00:10:13,320
Well, so remember, basic idea of contestable monopoly.

258
00:10:13,320 --> 00:10:15,720
Which firm takes over the markets?

259
00:10:15,720 --> 00:10:17,280
Whoever produces the cheapest.

260
00:10:17,280 --> 00:10:18,360
Yes, exactly.

261
00:10:18,360 --> 00:10:20,000
Whoever produces the cheapest.

262
00:10:20,000 --> 00:10:22,200
And where does that low cost producer

263
00:10:22,200 --> 00:10:24,280
wind up producing in this simple model?

264
00:10:24,280 --> 00:10:25,680
AC1.

265
00:10:25,680 --> 00:10:28,680
Right below AC2.

266
00:10:28,680 --> 00:10:31,200
So you go right below the cost of the second most

267
00:10:31,200 --> 00:10:32,760
efficient producer.

268
00:10:32,760 --> 00:10:38,280
So in that case, what we have is two things.

269
00:10:38,280 --> 00:10:40,560
So we have no productive inefficiency

270
00:10:40,560 --> 00:10:43,760
because it's the lowest cost producer producing.

271
00:10:43,760 --> 00:10:48,080
We are now, society is producing at the minimum average cost.

272
00:10:48,080 --> 00:10:51,560
We still have some allocated inefficiency over here.

273
00:10:51,560 --> 00:10:54,160
So remember that with differences in cost

274
00:10:54,160 --> 00:10:57,840
between the producers, the first most efficient producer

275
00:10:57,840 --> 00:11:00,440
produces it just below the cost of the second most

276
00:11:00,440 --> 00:11:01,680
efficient one.

277
00:11:01,680 --> 00:11:02,760
From the point of view of consumers,

278
00:11:02,760 --> 00:11:04,200
it doesn't change very much.

279
00:11:04,200 --> 00:11:05,800
But from the point of view of society,

280
00:11:05,800 --> 00:11:08,040
there's a big change, which is all of this stuff which

281
00:11:08,040 --> 00:11:11,880
was waste before gets turned into profits for the most

282
00:11:11,880 --> 00:11:13,960
efficient firm.

283
00:11:13,960 --> 00:11:15,800
And then we still do have that thing there.

284
00:11:15,800 --> 00:11:17,440
So that's there either way.

285
00:11:17,440 --> 00:11:19,640
But then it becomes, instead of having

286
00:11:19,640 --> 00:11:23,240
the whole trapezoid of productive inefficiency,

287
00:11:23,240 --> 00:11:28,480
we have the small triangle of allocated inefficiency.

288
00:11:28,480 --> 00:11:30,680
Now on the other hand, you can imagine

289
00:11:30,680 --> 00:11:34,480
a inefficient monopoly producing there, but with a price cap.

290
00:11:34,480 --> 00:11:36,960
Which is actually pretty common for government monopolies.

291
00:11:36,960 --> 00:11:38,520
So there's a government monopoly where

292
00:11:38,520 --> 00:11:41,480
they pick some crummy firm to be the only producer.

293
00:11:41,480 --> 00:11:44,040
And then they say, well, we have to go and protect consumers.

294
00:11:44,040 --> 00:11:45,400
So they're not allowed to make profits.

295
00:11:45,400 --> 00:11:47,040
Or they'll have a great return regulation

296
00:11:47,040 --> 00:11:48,000
or something like that.

297
00:11:48,000 --> 00:11:50,000
Think about the post office.

298
00:11:50,000 --> 00:11:51,620
Do you often hear about the post office

299
00:11:51,620 --> 00:11:53,440
making enormous profits?

300
00:11:53,440 --> 00:11:54,120
No.

301
00:11:54,120 --> 00:11:56,920
But it's illegal to compete with the post office

302
00:11:56,920 --> 00:11:58,360
for first class mail.

303
00:11:58,360 --> 00:12:03,280
And the very fact that they zealously guard this monopoly

304
00:12:03,280 --> 00:12:04,520
is kind of suspicious.

305
00:12:04,520 --> 00:12:05,980
What does it sound like they think

306
00:12:05,980 --> 00:12:08,200
would happen if anyone else could compete and deliver

307
00:12:08,200 --> 00:12:09,360
first class mail?

308
00:12:09,360 --> 00:12:10,480
Several people would enter.

309
00:12:10,480 --> 00:12:12,840
Yeah, it sounds like they'd need to be entered.

310
00:12:12,840 --> 00:12:15,080
Now, this does give the post office

311
00:12:15,080 --> 00:12:17,000
a great piece of propaganda, which

312
00:12:17,000 --> 00:12:22,840
is we are not hurting consumers because we have no profits.

313
00:12:22,840 --> 00:12:25,240
How could you possibly say we are in any way

314
00:12:25,240 --> 00:12:30,440
injuring you postal consumers when we don't make a dime?

315
00:12:30,440 --> 00:12:33,400
And how good is that argument?

316
00:12:33,400 --> 00:12:34,960
Well, first of all, persuasive.

317
00:12:34,960 --> 00:12:35,800
Very persuasive.

318
00:12:35,800 --> 00:12:38,480
How intellectually sound is that argument?

319
00:12:38,480 --> 00:12:39,280
Not very.

320
00:12:39,280 --> 00:12:40,920
It's terrible, right?

321
00:12:40,920 --> 00:12:44,520
So imagine that you're stuck with a firm that

322
00:12:44,520 --> 00:12:48,160
has such high costs that their costs are 10 times what

323
00:12:48,160 --> 00:12:51,720
they would that of the most efficient producer.

324
00:12:51,720 --> 00:12:54,640
So in that case, if you had competition,

325
00:12:54,640 --> 00:12:58,560
then suppose that we have a bunch of firms.

326
00:12:58,560 --> 00:13:00,280
We're all way up to AC10.

327
00:13:00,280 --> 00:13:03,880
Suppose that's the post office.

328
00:13:03,880 --> 00:13:05,840
And then if there were free competition,

329
00:13:05,840 --> 00:13:07,560
price would be driven all the way down

330
00:13:07,560 --> 00:13:13,280
to just below the second most efficient producer's costs.

331
00:13:13,280 --> 00:13:15,920
And then as a result of that, consumers,

332
00:13:15,920 --> 00:13:19,800
rather than getting them to pay that exploitation-less price,

333
00:13:19,800 --> 00:13:22,080
where they don't mail, where they just ship with agents,

334
00:13:22,080 --> 00:13:24,640
they sell at cost, but they're so terrible at their job

335
00:13:24,640 --> 00:13:27,640
that their cost is super high and you get a bad deal.

336
00:13:27,640 --> 00:13:30,160
Or you could go and pay a monopoly price

337
00:13:30,160 --> 00:13:32,080
bought to a deficient producer.

338
00:13:32,080 --> 00:13:35,120
And which one of these is better for consumers?

339
00:13:35,120 --> 00:13:37,000
That one where the producer makes no profit,

340
00:13:37,000 --> 00:13:38,880
or that one where they make a lot of profit?

341
00:13:38,880 --> 00:13:41,240
The one where they make a lot of profit.

342
00:13:41,240 --> 00:13:46,840
Because the key reason why zero something is wrong

343
00:13:46,840 --> 00:13:49,720
is the fact that the producer is making a profit automatically

344
00:13:49,720 --> 00:13:50,920
better for you.

345
00:13:50,920 --> 00:13:53,000
Depends on what his costs are.

346
00:13:53,000 --> 00:13:55,000
If he has low costs and he makes a modest profit,

347
00:13:55,000 --> 00:13:57,480
far better for you than if he had enormous costs

348
00:13:57,480 --> 00:14:02,200
and made no profit.

349
00:14:02,200 --> 00:14:03,680
Make sense?

350
00:14:03,680 --> 00:14:05,120
OK.

351
00:14:05,120 --> 00:14:05,600
All right.

352
00:14:05,600 --> 00:14:07,800
So anyway, in this world where we just

353
00:14:07,800 --> 00:14:10,760
charge a price equal to cost, but it's inefficient,

354
00:14:10,760 --> 00:14:12,400
but the producer's inefficient, then we

355
00:14:12,400 --> 00:14:14,880
have a little bit of a law of productive inefficiency,

356
00:14:14,880 --> 00:14:18,800
but with no allocator efficiency.

357
00:14:18,800 --> 00:14:20,640
Or of course, you could have both.

358
00:14:20,640 --> 00:14:28,640
So if you had, here's the lowest average cost,

359
00:14:28,640 --> 00:14:32,720
and here's the average cost of the actual producer.

360
00:14:32,720 --> 00:14:35,760
So if this was given to the brother-in-law of the dictator

361
00:14:35,760 --> 00:14:39,440
and he wanted to make money, what he would do is,

362
00:14:39,440 --> 00:14:42,600
I'm just going to look at his regular marginal revenue curve,

363
00:14:42,600 --> 00:14:45,360
and then he went producing something like here.

364
00:14:45,360 --> 00:14:50,800
And then we'd have that allocative inefficiency triangle

365
00:14:50,800 --> 00:14:53,440
and that productive inefficiency trapezoid.

366
00:14:53,440 --> 00:14:57,440
Then finally, we have that little rectangle of profit.

367
00:14:57,440 --> 00:15:00,400
So the result of all of these restrictions on competition

368
00:15:00,400 --> 00:15:03,800
is that to go and give this crummy producer

369
00:15:03,800 --> 00:15:06,720
that small rectangle, you have to go and take away

370
00:15:06,720 --> 00:15:20,080
this giant thing from consumers.

371
00:15:20,080 --> 00:15:22,360
And again, some obvious examples of things like this.

372
00:15:22,360 --> 00:15:26,040
Suppose that you ban Walmart to protect mom and pop stores.

373
00:15:26,040 --> 00:15:29,480
And standard story, why is it that Walmart does so well

374
00:15:29,480 --> 00:15:32,680
competing with mom and pop stores?

375
00:15:32,680 --> 00:15:37,120
Because Walmart has lower costs, much lower costs.

376
00:15:37,120 --> 00:15:39,080
It's not like you go to Walmart and say, my god,

377
00:15:39,080 --> 00:15:41,120
they're making tons of profits because the prices are so high

378
00:15:41,120 --> 00:15:41,640
here.

379
00:15:41,640 --> 00:15:43,080
It's like, well, why did you ever

380
00:15:43,080 --> 00:15:45,400
leave the wonderful mom and pop stores?

381
00:15:45,400 --> 00:15:47,480
Instead, the problem is the mom and pop stores,

382
00:15:47,480 --> 00:15:49,880
though they are often on the edge of bankruptcy,

383
00:15:49,880 --> 00:15:54,560
have such high costs that they can be simultaneously

384
00:15:54,560 --> 00:15:59,680
charging exorbitant prices and be on the edge of bankruptcy.

385
00:15:59,680 --> 00:16:02,040
Anyone ever here tried to run a business that you should

386
00:16:02,040 --> 00:16:04,320
identify with this?

387
00:16:04,320 --> 00:16:05,840
Running a business is really hard.

388
00:16:05,840 --> 00:16:09,200
And you can be saying, wow, my costs

389
00:16:09,200 --> 00:16:11,600
are so enormous that I have trouble making money,

390
00:16:11,600 --> 00:16:14,880
even if I do charge a high price, which is also

391
00:16:14,880 --> 00:16:17,760
hard to get people to do that.

392
00:16:17,760 --> 00:16:20,240
Now, this brings us to a very interesting difference

393
00:16:20,240 --> 00:16:23,120
between government and market monopolies.

394
00:16:23,120 --> 00:16:24,920
So in both government and market monopolies,

395
00:16:24,920 --> 00:16:28,600
you've got opportunities for allocation and efficiency.

396
00:16:28,600 --> 00:16:30,120
So whether it's a private monopoly

397
00:16:30,120 --> 00:16:32,120
that wants to raise price above marginal cost

398
00:16:32,120 --> 00:16:33,440
or a government monopoly that wants

399
00:16:33,440 --> 00:16:34,880
to raise price above marginal cost,

400
00:16:34,880 --> 00:16:37,360
either way you'll get allocated and efficiency.

401
00:16:37,360 --> 00:16:40,400
But government monopolies have a strong tendency

402
00:16:40,400 --> 00:16:44,600
to have the productive inefficiency trapezoid.

403
00:16:44,600 --> 00:16:47,280
Strong tendency on multiple levels.

404
00:16:47,280 --> 00:16:49,280
So one is, suppose they just pick the firm that

405
00:16:49,280 --> 00:16:51,000
gets to be the monopoly at random.

406
00:16:51,000 --> 00:16:53,040
What are the odds that they will pick the one that

407
00:16:53,040 --> 00:16:55,640
has the lowest costs?

408
00:16:55,640 --> 00:16:56,680
Really low.

409
00:16:56,680 --> 00:16:59,440
There's an enormous number of firms out in the world.

410
00:16:59,440 --> 00:17:02,000
And then you can add on some political economy.

411
00:17:02,000 --> 00:17:04,360
And what kinds of firms are most likely to come crying

412
00:17:04,360 --> 00:17:06,240
and saying, I need help?

413
00:17:06,240 --> 00:17:08,720
Probably inefficient ones.

414
00:17:08,720 --> 00:17:11,400
You may have heard, this is a good line, so remember it,

415
00:17:11,400 --> 00:17:13,320
if you haven't heard it.

416
00:17:13,320 --> 00:17:15,240
So in history text, folks will talk a lot

417
00:17:15,240 --> 00:17:18,360
about the infant industry argument for protectionism.

418
00:17:18,360 --> 00:17:20,480
And when you look at the actual world,

419
00:17:20,480 --> 00:17:22,840
infant industries are rarely protected.

420
00:17:22,840 --> 00:17:25,080
The ones that are much more often protected

421
00:17:25,080 --> 00:17:27,640
are what economists call senile industries,

422
00:17:27,640 --> 00:17:30,920
industries that are being driven to the wall, that

423
00:17:30,920 --> 00:17:34,480
are big but in decline.

424
00:17:34,480 --> 00:17:36,880
So it's the senile industries that are more likely to be

425
00:17:36,880 --> 00:17:39,200
protected, and this makes sense in terms of public choice.

426
00:17:39,200 --> 00:17:41,520
Because what kinds of industries are most likely

427
00:17:41,520 --> 00:17:44,160
to have a whole bunch of people around to go and complain

428
00:17:44,160 --> 00:17:46,320
and get favorable treatments?

429
00:17:46,320 --> 00:17:47,960
Industries that don't exist yet?

430
00:17:47,960 --> 00:17:50,360
How many people around in industries that don't exist yet

431
00:17:50,360 --> 00:17:52,600
complain?

432
00:17:52,600 --> 00:17:53,680
They don't exist yet?

433
00:17:53,680 --> 00:17:55,360
There's maybe a few visionaries.

434
00:17:55,360 --> 00:17:57,920
Wouldn't it be great if you would go and support

435
00:17:57,920 --> 00:17:59,880
an industry that doesn't exist yet?

436
00:17:59,880 --> 00:18:01,840
Pretty hard case to make.

437
00:18:01,840 --> 00:18:03,400
But on the other hand, for an industry

438
00:18:03,400 --> 00:18:06,520
that has employed 1% of the population,

439
00:18:06,520 --> 00:18:08,600
that sounds like one that is ideally

440
00:18:08,600 --> 00:18:13,600
suited to complain if, say, human-driven big rig trucks

441
00:18:13,600 --> 00:18:16,960
are going to be out-competed by rivalrous trucks.

442
00:18:20,640 --> 00:18:23,400
So key difference between private and public monopolies

443
00:18:23,400 --> 00:18:25,520
is the tendency for very high costs.

444
00:18:25,520 --> 00:18:27,200
And there's some really neat work

445
00:18:27,200 --> 00:18:30,160
by Congress John Ben-Renin and some co-authors

446
00:18:30,160 --> 00:18:32,280
where they look at management quality

447
00:18:32,280 --> 00:18:33,920
of different kinds of companies.

448
00:18:33,920 --> 00:18:38,480
And they reach a bunch of results which vary on.

449
00:18:38,480 --> 00:18:40,880
You'd be very much expected by people at GMU,

450
00:18:40,880 --> 00:18:43,280
but since they have no GMU associations,

451
00:18:43,280 --> 00:18:45,200
they've gotten a lot more attention for them.

452
00:18:45,200 --> 00:18:47,000
Of course, if you just want truth for bail,

453
00:18:47,000 --> 00:18:49,440
it's better if people other than us

454
00:18:49,440 --> 00:18:50,800
can be the vessels for the truth,

455
00:18:50,800 --> 00:18:52,400
can be the messengers, because they'll

456
00:18:52,400 --> 00:18:54,320
be believed, so one of the results

457
00:18:54,320 --> 00:18:56,280
is that government firms are poorly managed.

458
00:18:58,800 --> 00:19:00,360
Government firms are poorly managed.

459
00:19:00,360 --> 00:19:03,480
And as we know, poorly managed house

460
00:19:03,480 --> 00:19:05,960
makes you things like pay for performance

461
00:19:05,960 --> 00:19:08,560
and keeping track of productivity

462
00:19:08,560 --> 00:19:10,200
and making sure people show up on time

463
00:19:10,200 --> 00:19:13,360
and actually do their job, really common sense things.

464
00:19:13,360 --> 00:19:16,160
And as a result, government-owned firms

465
00:19:16,160 --> 00:19:18,200
have higher costs.

466
00:19:18,200 --> 00:19:19,520
Not too surprising.

467
00:19:19,520 --> 00:19:23,200
And then another neat result is basically

468
00:19:23,200 --> 00:19:25,640
the poorer the country, the worse the management generally

469
00:19:25,640 --> 00:19:26,520
is.

470
00:19:26,520 --> 00:19:28,720
And therefore, in poor third world countries,

471
00:19:28,720 --> 00:19:30,600
those firms are generally very poorly managed

472
00:19:30,600 --> 00:19:32,920
with horrible nepotism and just not keeping

473
00:19:32,920 --> 00:19:34,560
track of what's happening.

474
00:19:34,560 --> 00:19:36,960
But there is one great exception to the rule

475
00:19:36,960 --> 00:19:40,560
that companies in third world are poorly managed.

476
00:19:40,560 --> 00:19:43,800
There's a general category of firm that is well managed.

477
00:19:43,800 --> 00:19:45,960
And they're basically managed at first world levels

478
00:19:45,960 --> 00:19:47,920
in the third world.

479
00:19:47,920 --> 00:19:50,480
Multinational corporations.

480
00:19:50,480 --> 00:19:52,880
So to say it like you're doing a trailer for a movie,

481
00:19:52,880 --> 00:19:55,640
here's multinational corporations.

482
00:19:55,640 --> 00:20:18,880
But once again, coming back to productive inefficiency.


